## What was missing and what I did

1. CLAUDE.md (`doc_sync: FAIL`) — the more serious of the two. Line 221 still read *"Two findings recorded and **FILED rather than fixed"* about #859, so the file re-injected as authoritative context every session asserted the defect was open** while the code fixed it. That's the superseded-claim shape this repo records as having cost eight sessions on #683.

Following house convention, I recorded rather than erased: round 87's original sentence stays intact as the record of what that round did, with the correction beside it noting it is *"false as a description of the code"*. The new text documents the anchored-prefix-vs-ANSI mechanism, the Pattern-3 `contains()` asymmetry, the one-site application (and why per-pattern would have restored the error but still lost file:line via extract_location), the measured scope, and — stated rather than implied — what is not covered.

2. Follow-up issue → [#861](https://github.com/yologdev/yoyo-evolve/issues/861) for the unswept parse_typescript_errors / parse_python_errors.

3. The code comment naming the siblings — an explicit step-4 requirement (*"Name them in the code comment **and* file ONE follow-up issue") that the evaluator didn't list but was also absent. Added to the stripper's doc comment.

## One judgment call worth flagging

I checked how the siblings anchor before writing the issue rather than assuming: both use strip_prefix/starts_with on trimmed lines, so they are structurally exposed to the same mechanism. But I have **no captured fixture for `tsc`/`eslint`/`pytest`/`mypy`, so both the issue and the code comment say structurally present, not empirically confirmed** — and specifically note that several tools disable colour on a pipe by default, which is exactly why it's worth checking rather than assuming (cargo doesn't, which is why #859 was real). Claiming those parsers are broken would be inventing a hole.

